WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:01.780
In the mood for a good laugh?

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:05.629
Well, you could always check out some big-budget
comedy everybody's seen a million times, or

00:00:05.629 --> 00:00:09.429
you could dig a bit deeper and find something
funny that's been swept under the cinematic

00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:09.980
rug.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.080
If you want to try something a bit more obscure,
keep watching to discover some hysterical

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.080
comedies that flew completely under the radar.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.420
After Hours

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:23.220
In 1985, legendary director Martin Scorsese
created one of the weirdest comedies to ever

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:27.599
come from a mainstream filmmaker, an oddball
movie called After Hours.

00:00:27.599 --> 00:00:31.099
Griffin Dunne plays a guy named Paul Hackett
who just wants to get a ride home after a

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:32.550
date in New York City.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:37.190
But instead, he finds himself lost in a twisted
Wizard of Oz universe version of the Big Apple

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:40.039
where everyone else seems to have gone completely
bonkers.

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:44.420
It all plays out like an eerily funny fever
dream or some long lost Lewis Carroll novel

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:48.399
where there are no rules and you should avoid
everyone you meet because they're all stark,

00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:50.110
raving mad.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:51.320
Withnail and I

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.649
Comedy isn't always just about the laughs.

00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:59.129
For proof, look no further than 1987's Withnail
and I, a dark British comedy that reeks of

00:00:59.129 --> 00:01:00.850
weed, wine, and desperation.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.800
Paul McGann and Richard E. Grant play two
struggling actors who take a vacation in the

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.700
country to get their heads straight.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:11.670
Filled with quotable lines and hilarious hijinks,
Withnail and I is also a deeply sad film about

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:16.190
struggling with addiction and finding yourself
trapped in life with nowhere to go but down.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:19.950
Don't worry, though, it's still funny, with
Time Out London ranking it at number 7 on

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.620
its countdown of the 100 best comedies ever
made.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:24.720
The Brothers Bloom

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.730
Rian Johnson is best known for films like
Brick, Looper, and Star Wars: The Last Jedi.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:34.130
But he's also responsible for the criminally
underrated comedy The Brothers Bloom, a delightful

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:38.070
romp full of confidence schemes, double crosses,
and a lot of nitroglycerin.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:42.490
Narrated by the great Ricky Jay, The Brothers
Bloom might be a bit too quirky for some,

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:46.880
but if you don't mind the fantastical style,
stars Mark Ruffalo, Adrien Brody, and Rachel

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.910
Weisz deliver performances that are incredibly
fun to watch.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:53.750
Plus, like any good con movie, it does a great
job of constantly making you wonder if you're

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.130
in on the game or if you're actually being
played.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:58.130
MacGruber

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:02.890
Inspired by the Saturday Night Live sketch,
MacGruber finds Will Forte sporting a mullet,

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:06.500
saving America, and finding weird new ways
to use celery sticks.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:11.190
The film was a massive flop when it came out
in 2010 and was eviscerated by critics, but

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:15.500
in the years since, this send-up of 1980s
action flicks has found a cult following.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:20.570
Even Christopher Nolan is a fan, telling Business
Insider there are scenes that had him "howling

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:21.570
uncontrollably."

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:26.560
Raunchy, bloody, and stupid in equal measures,
this one is simply classic MacGruber.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:27.420
"Who's first?"

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.400
"Aw, s---!"

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.500
Tucker and Dale vs. Evil

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:38.100
If the horror genre has taught us anything,
it's that you can't trust a hillbilly.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.960
It seems these backwoods bumpkins are always
looking for preppy kids to chop into tiny

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:42.960
pieces, right?

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.130
Well, 2010's Tucker and Dale vs. Evil flips
this idea on its head, turning the mountain

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:51.240
men into lovable lugs just trying to help
a group of vacationing college kids who are

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.460
so terrified by horror movie stereotypes about
rednecks that they keep accidentally killing

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:56.460
themselves.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.420
Not only is Tucker and Dale vs. Evil hilarious,
it actually has an important message.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.160
Good job, guys!

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.000
Everybody Wants Some!!

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:08.580
Directed by Richard Linklater, 2016's Everybody
Wants Some!! has been hailed as the "spiritual

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:11.600
sequel" to Linklater's classic Dazed and Confused.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:16.100
Set in 1980, the film follows a group of college
baseball players in the days before class

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:17.100
starts.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.070
There's not really much of a plot, as the
film focuses instead on the relationships

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.990
between these jocks, competitive dudes who
spend their days guzzling beer, battling each

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:26.130
other, and bonding over ball games.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:30.180
It's extremely funny, while also examining
the mechanics of American masculinity.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.620
"What am I thinking about?"

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:33.620
"Baseball…"

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:35.420
"Having a tail."

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:36.440
"Cereal?"

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:37.880
"F---!"

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.020
Hunt for the Wilderpeople

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:45.280
Before signing on to direct Thor: Ragnarok,
New Zealand native Taika Waititi gave us brilliant

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.190
gems like What We Do in the Shadows.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:51.450
But the man really topped himself with 2016's
Hunt for the Wilderpeople, a story of two

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:55.630
misfits on the run from ninjas, dire wolves,
and an overzealous child welfare agent who

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:58.330
thinks she's the star of an American cop show.

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:01.940
Waititi's visuals are breathtaking, and the
film expertly jumps back and forth between

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.890
gut-busting comedy and tear-jerking sadness.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.560
In short, this is one majestical movie.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.420
"Once rejected.

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:11.520
Now accepted.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.380
By me.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.240
And Hector!

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.030
We're a trifecta!"

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.560
The Nice Guys

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.510
Written and directed by Shane Black, who redefined
the buddy cop film with Lethal Weapon, 2016's

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:28.600
The Nice Guys is one of the best entries in
the genre in recent memory, with stars Ryan

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.580
Gosling and Russell Crowe bouncing off each
other like bloody, boozy versions of Laurel

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:33.580
and Hardy.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:37.470
Whether they're getting into gunfights, interrogating
witnesses, or discussing near-death experiences

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.970
involving Richard Nixon, Gosling and Crowe
make the perfect comedy team, and really,

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.410
we totally wouldn't mind seeing these actors
reunite somewhere down the line.

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:47.340
That would actually be pretty nice, guys.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.000
Popstar: Never Stop Never Stopping

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.561
Straight from the incredible minds of The
Lonely Island, 2016's Popstar: Never Stop

00:05:00.561 --> 00:05:05.180
Never Stopping attempts to do for pop music
what This Is Spinal Tap did for heavy metal.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:10.660
And it succeeds, delivering a spot on, hilarious
mockumentary featuring Andy Samberg as a former

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:14.841
boy band star who finds success as a solo
act, only to learn being a loner isn't all

00:05:14.841 --> 00:05:15.990
it's cracked up to be.

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:20.680
Jam-packed with some fantastically funny tunes,
it also features cameos from musicians like

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.680
Usher, Mariah Carey, and an amazing Justin
Timberlake.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:25.889
"My name is Mr. Fish!"

00:05:25.889 --> 00:05:26.700
"Yeah!"

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.060
"These guys thoughts have granted my wish!

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:29.240
Oh yeah!"

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:32.500
"All I wanna know is who's in that fish costume,
that was amazing!"

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:33.949
"The fish was me!"

00:05:33.949 --> 00:05:37.800
By the time the credits roll, we guarantee
you'll want to jump out of your seat and start

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.070
doing the Donkey Roll.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:40.070
Thanks for watching!

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:42.370
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.940
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

